{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784647570,"nonce":"uqpuYxebsTLkuxvrx8Xp2Fi4088=","message":"## Root-cause analysis\n\nTwo independent gaps produce the awkward flow: the upgrade action is gated\nto states that exclude `waiting`, and the \"newer version available\" cue is\nconfined to the detail panel.\n\n**1. Upgrade unavailable while `waiting`.** The action-menu builder offers\n`('upgrade', …)` only when `status in ('reviewing', 'partial')` and\n`has_newer` (`src/b4/review_tui/_tracking_app.py:1358-1361`). A `waiting`\nseries gets only `('review', 'Review')` (line 1363). The handler enforces\nthe same floor: `action_update_revision` bails unless\n`status in ('reviewing', 'new')` — \"Series must be checked out or new to\nupgrade\" (`_tracking_app.py:3781-3785`). So a paused/`waiting` series — the\nexact state you're in while awaiting a new version — cannot upgrade\ndirectly; you must `review` first, then upgrade. There is no combined\n\"upgrade and return to reviewing\" transition; the two are separate by\nconstruction.\n\nDesign tension worth flagging: `waiting` is *intended* to auto-upgrade on\n`[u]pdate` — the detail hint reads \"will auto-upgrade on update\"\n(`_tracking_app.py:2052-2055`) and update-time promotion handles it. But\nthat implicit path isn't what Mark expects; he wants an explicit action.\n\n**2. No list-level highlight.** `has_newer` is computed in `_load_series`\n(`_tracking_app.py:1032`) but consumed *only* by `_show_details`\n(lines 2045-2063), where it toggles the `has-upgrade` CSS class on\n`#detail-revisions` (CSS at line 681). The main series-list row builder\n(`SeriesRow.compose`, ~530-620) never reads `has_newer`, so a series with a\nnewer version looks identical to every other row — you only discover the\nupgrade after selecting the series and reading the detail panel. There is\nalso no filter/view that lists \"series with upgrades available.\"\n\n## Related bugs\n\n`cc3f07d` is the `partial` sibling of gap 1 — the menu *offers* upgrade but\nthe handler refuses it; same guard family. `47d5a4c` / `2235355` cover how\na newer version gets *discovered* in the first place (upstream of this UX).","files":null}]}